# Trivy suppressions. Read by every trivy-action step in docker-publish.yml and
# trivy-scheduled.yml — Trivy auto-detects `.trivyignore` in the working directory, which is
# the repo root after each workflow's `actions/checkout`. That means these entries drop out of
# the SARIF inventory too, not just the gates, which is a deliberate narrowing of the
# "Security tab gets the complete inventory" rule those workflows otherwise follow. The comment
# at each SARIF step records the amendment.
#
# ONLY for findings that can never clear, because upstream declines them as vulnerabilities.
# Nothing merely unfixed-for-now belongs here: a CVE Debian has postponed is exactly the
# awareness the scheduled scan exists to produce, and hiding it would defeat the
# distroless-vs-Chainguard trade-off this alerting was budgeted for.
#
# Admission test — all three, verified on the Debian security tracker, not assumed:
#   1. tagged <unimportant> for trixie (NOT <no-dsa> and NOT <postponed>), and
#   2. "vulnerable" in sid/forky too, i.e. no fix is in flight anywhere, and
#   3. an upstream statement declining it as a security issue.
# A <no-dsa> or <postponed> tag fails the test even when the severity looks trivial.
#
# A FINDING THAT FAILS THIS TEST IS NOT HOMELESS. `.cve-acknowledged` is the sibling file for a
# finding that IS a vulnerability and HAS been ruled on — it carries an id, an expiry and a
# ruling reference, and it stops a daily duplicate issue without touching any gate. The two
# answer different questions, and mixing them up is how a postponed CVE ends up silenced here:
#   .trivyignore      is this a vulnerability at all?  (upstream declined it; it can never clear)
#   .cve-acknowledged has a human already ruled on it?  (it is real, and the ruling expires)
#
# Everything below is glibc, all LOW, all triaged 2026-08-02 against
# https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/source-package/glibc with installed
# libc6 2.41-12+deb13u3 (identical to trixie's current version — nothing to pull).
# Re-verify before adding a line; do not extend this file by pattern-matching on severity.

# "That's standard POSIX behaviour implemented by (e)glibc. Applications using glob need to
# impose limits for themselves." — Debian. glob() resource consumption; not a glibc defect.
CVE-2010-4756

# Rejected under glibc's published Security Exceptions policy
# (https://sourceware.org/glibc/wiki/Security%20Exceptions). Stack exhaustion in a
# non-security context. sourceware bug 34141 / gnulib thread.
CVE-2018-20796

# The four 2019-1010xxx "mitigation bypass" reports, each marked "Not treated as a security
# issue by upstream" by Debian, each with a sourceware bug closed on that basis. They describe
# defeating hardening from a position that already implies compromise.
CVE-2019-1010022 # sourceware 22850
CVE-2019-1010023 # sourceware 22851
CVE-2019-1010024 # sourceware 22852
CVE-2019-1010025 # sourceware 22853

# check_dst_limits_calc_pos_1 stack exhaustion in the regex engine; sourceware bug 24269,
# unimportant for Debian, still "vulnerable" in sid seven years on.
CVE-2019-9192
